---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const glucoseCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'gfr*x/100', label: 'filtered load (mg/min)', color: '#1d4ed8' },
    { expr: 'max(0, gfr*(x-180)/100)', label: 'excreted in urine (mg/min)', color: '#b42318' },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'gfr', label: 'GFR', min: 100, max: 150, step: 5, value: 125, unit: 'mL/min' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 400, label: 'plasma glucose (mg/dL)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 600, label: 'mg per minute' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const labCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'gfr', label: 'GFR', min: 100, max: 150, step: 5, value: 125, unit: 'mL/min' },
    { name: 're', label: 'water reabsorbed', min: 98, max: 99.5, step: 0.1, value: 99.4, unit: '%' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'filtrate formed per day', expr: 'gfr*1440/1000', unit: 'L/day' },
    { label: 'urine produced per day', expr: 'gfr*1440/1000*(1 - re/100)', unit: 'L/day' },
  ],
};

const nephronCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'excess amino acids arrive at the liver', note: 'Protein you eat but do not build with cannot be stored — the surplus amino acids must be dealt with.' },
    { math: 'deamination: the NH₂ group is removed', note: 'The liver strips off the nitrogen-containing part of each amino acid, leaving a carbohydrate residue that can be respired or converted.' },
    { math: 'the nitrogen part → urea', note: 'The amine group first forms ammonia — highly toxic — which the liver converts to urea, far less toxic and far less water-hungry to transport.' },
    { math: 'glomerulus: ultrafiltration of water, glucose, urea, ions', note: 'Blood pressure squeezes small molecules out of the capillary knot into the nephron; blood cells and proteins stay behind.' },
    { math: 'nephron: all glucose + some ions + most water reabsorbed', note: 'Selective reabsorption hauls back everything worth keeping. What remains — urea, excess water, excess ions — is urine.' },
    { math: 'urine: urea + excess water + excess ions → bladder', note: 'Collecting ducts deliver it to the renal pelvis, down the ureters, into the bladder — a variable mix, tuned minute by minute.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/intermediate/excretion-in-humans">
  <h2>The idea: taking out the rubbish</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Excretion</strong> is the removal from the body of the waste products of
    metabolism — not undigested food, which leaves by a different door. Two organs do almost
    all of it: the <strong>lungs</strong> excrete carbon dioxide, and the <strong>kidneys</strong>
    excrete <strong>urea, excess water and excess ions</strong>. Why bother? Because urea is
    toxic: let it accumulate in the blood and it poisons cells. Water and salt balance matter
    just as much — too much or too little dissolved stuff and your cells stop working. A drop of
    plasma makes about 60 round trips through the kidneys every day; this lesson follows it
    through.
  </p>

  <h2>Where urea comes from: the liver</h2>
  <p>
    Urea is made, not filtered straight from food. Amino acids you cannot use are taken to the
    <strong>liver</strong>, which performs <strong>deamination</strong>: the nitrogen-containing
    part is removed and converted to urea, while the rest is assimilated — built back into
    proteins or respired. The urea then travels in the blood to the kidneys. The plumbing is
    simple: two <strong>kidneys</strong>, each with an outer <strong>cortex</strong> and inner
    <strong>medulla</strong>, two <strong>ureters</strong> draining to the <strong>bladder</strong>,
    and a <strong>urethra</strong> out. The kidney is fed by a renal artery straight from the
    aorta and drained by a renal vein — a quarter of your cardiac output passes through the two
    organs at any moment, roughly 1.2 litres of blood per minute through fists-sized filters.
    The kidney's energy bill is correspondingly serious: the active transport that drives
    reabsorption consumes on the order of 7% of the body's resting ATP — a small organ, roughly
    0.4% of body mass, running one of the most energetically demanding operations in the body.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is worth pausing on why the liver bothers with the two-step ammonia-to-urea detour.
    Ammonia is small and extremely soluble, but also extremely toxic — it would poison nerve
    cells at low concentrations and demands large volumes of water to flush. Urea is far less
    toxic, so it can ride the blood at useful concentrations without harming anything, and the
    kidneys can then choose how much water to spend excreting it. That trade-off — toxicity
    against water cost — is the same logic that shaped uric acid in birds and insects (safer
    still, but energetically dearer to make), and it is why excretion and water balance can
    never be discussed separately.
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="from surplus protein to concentrated urine — the whole route in six steps" widget="equationStep" config={nephronCfg} />

  <h2>The nephron: filter, then re-choose</h2>
  <p>
    Each kidney packs about a million <strong>nephrons</strong>, and each one works in two
    moves. First, <strong>ultrafiltration</strong>: blood pressure in the <strong>glomerulus</strong>
    — a knot of capillaries fed by a wide inflow and drained by a narrow outflow, so pressure
    builds like a kinked garden hose — forces water, glucose, urea, ions and small amino acids
    out of the blood into the nephron's cup-shaped <strong>Bowman's capsule</strong>. The filter
    is three-layered: capillary wall slits, a basement membrane that is the real molecular
    sieve, and filtration slits between podocyte cells. Anything above roughly 68,000 in
    molecular mass — blood cells, platelets, almost all proteins — cannot pass. Second,
    <strong>selective reabsorption</strong>: as the filtrate flows the length of the tubule, the
    nephron takes back <strong>all of the glucose, some of the ions and most of the water</strong>.
    The proximal tubule does most of the hauling with active transport (its cells are packed
    with mitochondria and coated in microvilli — a thousandfold surface amplification); the
    loop of Henle and collecting duct finish the water balance. What remains — urea, excess
    water, excess ions — is urine. Notice what the two moves imply: the nephron never "decides"
    at the filter, only at the reabsorber. Urea's fate is instructive — about half of what is
    filtered is passively reabsorbed anyway, simply because water's exit concentrates it, but
    the rest is left in the tubule deliberately. The body keeps the waste it must excrete by
    never giving it a way back in.
  </p>
  <p>
    The graphs below show the system's safety margin — glucose is normally reabsorbed
    completely, and only appears in urine when plasma levels exceed the renal threshold of about
    <strong>180 mg/dL</strong>, as in untreated diabetes:
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="filtered load vs what is excreted — the curves separate only above the renal threshold (≈180 mg/dL)" widget="plotter" config={glucoseCfg} />

  <p>
    The scale of the filtering is hard to believe until you compute it. A GFR of
    <strong>125 mL per minute</strong> sounds small; over a day it is <strong>180 litres</strong>
    — about four times your blood volume, filtered and mostly reabsorbed. Every day your
    kidneys reabsorb roughly 179 litres of water, about 180 grams of glucose, and kilograms of
    ions — recycling the entire plasma volume dozens of times and losing only the surplus:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="180 L in, about 1–2 L out — the arithmetic of selective reabsorption" widget="sliderLab" config={labCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the slider lab as an experiment. At the default reabsorption of 99.4%, the day's urine
    output is about 1.1 litres. Drop reabsorption to 99.0% — barely a change you could measure
    directly — and urine output nearly triples to about 1.8 litres. That sensitivity is the
    whole point of the design: because almost everything is reabsorbed by default, tiny
    percentage changes in reabsorption give huge, precise changes in output. The kidney does not
    decide how much to excrete; it decides how much to <em>keep</em>, and the remainder follows.
  </p>

  <h2>ADH and osmoregulation: the water thermostat</h2>
  <p>
    Water balance is a negative-feedback loop (the neurobiology lesson's homeostasis machinery,
    applied). <strong>ADH</strong> (antidiuretic hormone) is released by the pituitary when
    blood becomes too concentrated — detected by osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus — and makes
    the collecting duct more permeable to water, so more is reabsorbed and the urine becomes
    concentrated and small in volume. Drink a litre of water and ADH secretion switches off;
    pale, copious urine follows within the hour. The loop closes: any deviation in blood
    concentration automatically calls up its own correction. Alcohol suppresses ADH release,
    which is why beer dehydrates while it appears to hydrate — the water leaves, minus the
    hormone that would have kept it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the kidney filters the blood like a sieve for dirt." The
    glomerulus does not select what to remove — it filters <em>everything small</em>, including
    180 g of glucose a day that the body absolutely needs. Selectivity comes almost entirely
    from reabsorption. That inverted design is what gives the system its power: anything the
    blood carries that the tubule has no transporter for (most drugs, their metabolites, urea)
    is automatically excreted, no recognition required. You filter first and choose later —
    which is why the kidneys are both the body's cleanser and its best poison detector.</p>

  <h2>Reading urine: the cheapest diagnostic in medicine</h2>
  <p>
    Urine's normal composition tells you it is doing its job: about <strong>95% water</strong>,
    roughly <strong>2% urea</strong>, with creatinine, ions (sodium, chloride, potassium,
    phosphate) and traces of other metabolites making up the rest — no glucose, no protein, no
    blood cells. Every deviation is a diagnosis waiting to happen. <strong>Glucose</strong> in
    urine means plasma exceeded the renal threshold — the classic flag for diabetes (the
    plotter above shows exactly why the flag trips only above 180 mg/dL).
    <strong>Protein</strong> in urine means the glomerular filter itself is leaking — a marker
    of kidney damage, since healthy filters bar anything that big.
    <strong>Blood</strong> or pus cells point to infection, stones or injury elsewhere in the
    tract. Even colour and concentration carry information: pale urine means dilute blood and
    a wet recent history; dark, scanty urine means the ADH loop is squeezing hard — you are
    dehydrated and the kidney is protecting you. The dipstick test a nurse runs in thirty
    seconds is just selective reabsorption read backwards: whatever shows up in the urine is
    whatever the nephron failed to keep.
  </p>

  <h2>Kidney failure: dialysis and transplant</h2>
  <p>
    When nephrons fail — through diabetes, high blood pressure, infection or inherited disease —
    urea and ions climb and water balance collapses; untreated, the patient dies within days to
    weeks. The whole apparatus is dialysis by another name — the same partially permeable
    membranes you met in the osmosis lesson, run at blood pressure. A <strong>dialysis
    machine</strong> passes the patient's blood over a selectively permeable membrane against
    fresh dialysis fluid, letting urea and excess ions diffuse away while keeping cells and
    proteins in, doing for a few hours what a million nephrons do continuously. The fluid's
    composition is the therapeutic art: glucose at blood levels prevents sugar loss, ion levels
    set the direction of each ion's diffusion, and the dialysate is refreshed fast enough to
    keep urea's gradient steep. Typical schedules are three to four sessions a week, four hours
    each. <strong>Transplant</strong> a working kidney and the machine becomes unnecessary —
    but the recipient's immune system must be suppressed for life, because the new kidney is,
    genetically, somebody else's (the immune system lesson explains the stakes). One donor
    kidney, running at perhaps 60–80% of the filtration a pair would deliver, is enough — the
    system's safety margin was built for exactly this. Peritoneal dialysis, the portable
    alternative, uses the patient's own abdominal lining as the membrane and can run overnight:
    less efficient than a machine, but closer to the continuous, gentle correction a real
    kidney performs.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The loop of Henle is the deep mechanism behind concentrated urine, and it works by
    <strong>countercurrent multiplication</strong>. The loop dips into the medulla and transports
    salt actively out of its ascending limb, building an osmotic gradient in the surrounding
    tissue that increases from cortex to medulla — up to about 1,200 mOsm, four times blood
    concentration. Fluid descending the loop passes down this gradient; fluid ascending leaves
    salt behind; the hairpin geometry lets the two flows multiply each other's effect. The
    collecting duct then descends through the same gradient, and ADH decides how many of its
    water channels (aquaporins) are open: full open and the fluid equilibrates with the deep
    medulla — concentrated, small-volume urine; shut and water escapes — dilute, copious urine.
    Desert rodents with extreme medullas concentrate urine to over 20 times blood; humans manage
    about 4. The loop's length sets the ceiling — which is why anatomy is destiny in osmoregulation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Clinically, kidney function is measured, not guessed. The standard number is the GFR, and
    the clever way to measure it uses a molecule the kidney neither reabsorbs nor secretes —
    <strong>creatinine</strong> from muscle metabolism — so whatever is filtered is excreted;
    blood creatinine therefore rises as GFR falls, and a single blood test tracks the decline.
    "Chronic kidney disease" is staged on that number, and by the time symptoms appear, roughly
    two-thirds of function is usually gone — the safety margin that hides failure for years is
    also what delays its detection. Research frontiers include implantable artificial nephrons,
    regenerating nephrons from stem cells, and portable dialysis. Each is an attempt to rebuild
    in silicon or cells what evolution built from a million microscopic filters — and every
    design, biological or artificial, converges on the same two moves: filter everything, then
    take back what matters.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus (2026–2028), unit 13; OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology, ch. 25 (The Urinary System); Guyton &amp; Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, 14th ed. (GFR 125 mL/min, renal threshold).</p>
</TopicPage>
